\title{The Arithmetic of the Discoverable: Enumeration, Non-Exhaustibility, and the Physical Limits of Mathematical Discovery}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give a rigorous account of a phenomenon we call the \emph{finite/infinite scissor} in the theory of formal discovery. Fix a formal system over a finite alphabet whose set of provable statements (theorems) is infinite. We prove three complementary facts. (1) The set of theorems is countably infinite and admits an explicit, computable enumeration---the \emph{shortlex} enumeration---in which each theorem occupies a unique finite index. (2) Consequently each individual theorem has a well-defined finite \emph{discovery index}: a systematic search finds it after finitely many steps. (3) Nevertheless, for every finite budget $N$ the set of theorems discovered within $N$ steps is finite while the undiscovered set remains infinite; no finite process exhausts the theorems. We then connect this dichotomy to physics. Using standard bounds on physical computation (Landauer's principle, the Margolus--Levitin and Bremermann bounds) together with the finiteness of the free energy available over the future of the universe, we conclude that any physically realizable discovery process performs only finitely many enumeration steps and therefore discovers only finitely many of the countably infinitely many theorems. The heat death of the universe thus imposes a permanent, quantitative ceiling on mathematical \emph{access} while leaving mathematical \emph{truth} untouched. We close with enumeration-order optimality, cooperative multi-system discovery, and a resource-bounded incompleteness gap.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The question ``how much of mathematics can ever be known?'' is usually treated as philosophy. We treat it as a theorem-proving exercise with a physical corollary. The engine of the paper is a tension between two senses of \emph{finite}:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Per-object finiteness.} Every theorem is reached at a finite stage of a systematic search.
  \item \textbf{Collective non-finiteness.} No finite stage reaches every theorem.
\end{itemize}
These are not in conflict; they are two true facts about a single infinite list, and their interplay---the \emph{scissor}---determines how much of an infinite subject a finite process can uncover. When the finite process is a physical computer bounded by the free-energy budget of a universe headed toward heat death, the scissor becomes a statement about the ultimate reach of science.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\Sigma$ is a fixed finite alphabet with $|\Sigma| = k \ge 1$, equipped with a total (``alphabetical'') order.

\begin{definition}[Statements]
A \emph{statement} is a finite string over $\Sigma$. The set of all statements is $\Sigma^{*} = \bigcup_{\ell \ge 0} \Sigma^{\ell}$, where $\Sigma^{\ell}$ is the set of strings of length exactly $\ell$; note $|\Sigma^{\ell}| = k^{\ell} < \infty$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Formal system]
A \emph{formal system} $S$ is specified by axioms and a finite set of inference rules over $\Sigma$. Its \emph{theorem set} $T_S \subseteq \Sigma^{*}$ is the set of statements admitting a finite derivation. We assume $T_S$ is \emph{infinite}, as holds for any consistent system proving all instances of a nontrivial parameterized identity.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Shortlex order]
The \emph{shortlex} order $\preceq$ on $\Sigma^{*}$ is defined by $u \prec v$ iff $|u| < |v|$, or $|u| = |v|$ and $u$ precedes $v$ lexicographically.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Enumeration]
An \emph{enumeration} is a bijection $e : \N \to \Sigma^{*}$. The \emph{shortlex enumeration} $e_{\mathrm{sl}}$ is the unique enumeration order-preserving from $(\N,\le)$ to $(\Sigma^{*},\preceq)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Discovery index]
Fix an enumeration $e$. The \emph{index} of $\theta \in \Sigma^{*}$ is the unique $n$ with $e(n)=\theta$. For $\theta \in T_S$ this is the \emph{discovery index} $\idx_e(\theta)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Discovery frontier]
For a budget $N \in \N$, the \emph{discovered set} is $D_e(N) = \{\theta \in T_S : \idx_e(\theta) < N\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Natural density]
For $A \subseteq \Sigma^{*}$, the \emph{upper density} is $\overline{d}_e(A) = \limsup_{N} \tfrac1N |\{n<N : e(n)\in A\}|$; when the corresponding $\liminf$ agrees, the common value is the \emph{density} $d_e(A)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Core Results}

\begin{theorem}[Countability and enumeration]\label{thm:enum}
Let $S$ be a formal system over a finite alphabet $\Sigma$ with infinite theorem set $T_S$. Then $T_S$ is countably infinite, and the shortlex enumeration $e_{\mathrm{sl}} : \N \to \Sigma^{*}$ is a bijection under which each theorem occupies a unique finite index.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Sigma^{*} = \bigcup_\ell \Sigma^\ell$ is a countable union of finite sets, hence countable. The shortlex order is a well-order in which each string has only finitely many predecessors (all shorter strings, plus lexicographically earlier equal-length strings: $\sum_{j<\ell} k^j$ plus its rank within $\Sigma^\ell$). Mapping a string to its predecessor count is an order isomorphism $\Sigma^{*}\cong\N$ with inverse $e_{\mathrm{sl}}$. As $T_S\subseteq\Sigma^{*}$ is infinite and any infinite subset of a countable set is countably infinite, $T_S$ is countably infinite, with $\idx_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(\theta) = |\{s : s\prec\theta\}| < \infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence and uniqueness of discovery indices]\label{thm:idx}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:enum}, every $\theta \in T_S$ has a unique finite discovery index $\idx_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(\theta)$. If provability is semi-decidable, a dovetailing search prints $\theta$ after finitely many steps.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Finiteness and uniqueness follow from bijectivity of $e_{\mathrm{sl}}$. For the effective version, dovetail over pairs (statement, proof candidate) in shortlex order of their finite encoding and check validity; since $\theta$ has some finite proof $p_0$, the pair $(\theta,p_0)$ has finite index and is reached in finitely many steps.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-exhaustibility]\label{thm:nonexh}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:enum}, for every finite $N \in \N$:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $D_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(N)$ is finite, with $|D_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(N)| \le N$;
  \item $T_S \setminus D_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(N)$ is infinite.
\end{enumerate}
No finite budget exhausts $T_S$, while $|D_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(N)| \to \infty$ as $N \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$N$ steps inspect $e_{\mathrm{sl}}(0),\dots,e_{\mathrm{sl}}(N-1)$, so $|D_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(N)|\le N$. As $T_S$ is infinite and $D_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(N)$ finite, the complement is infinite. Since $e_{\mathrm{sl}}$ is a bijection onto an infinite $T_S$-containing space, arbitrarily large indices are theorems, so $|D_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(N)|$ is unbounded.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{rem:density}
Non-exhaustibility ($|D(N)|\to\infty$) does not require positive density: $T_S$ may have density zero, so $|D_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(N)|/N \to 0$ while $|D_{e_{\mathrm{sl}}}(N)|\to\infty$. ``Discovered infinitely often'' and ``discovered with positive frequency'' are independent; the scissor uses only the former.
\end{remark}

\section{The Physical Ceiling}

We use three standard bounds. \emph{Landauer's principle}: erasing one bit at temperature $\Theta$ dissipates at least $k_B \Theta \ln 2$. \emph{Margolus--Levitin}: a system of average energy $E$ above its ground state passes through at most $2E/(\pi\hbar)$ orthogonal states per second. \emph{Bremermann's limit}: at most $\approx 1.36\times10^{50}$ operations per second per kilogram of mass-energy.

\begin{definition}[Budget function]
A physical discovery process is modeled by a nondecreasing budget function $B : \Rnn \to \N$, where $B(t)$ is the maximum number of enumeration steps completed by time $t$.
\end{definition}

\begin{hypothesis}[Finite lifetime budget]\label{hyp:finite}
The total budget $B_\infty := \lim_{t\to\infty} B(t) = \sup_t B(t)$ is finite.
\end{hypothesis}

Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:finite} is supported by two independent estimates: a retrospective total-operations bound of order $10^{120}$ for the observable universe since the Big Bang, and a prospective free-energy-exhaustion argument, since irreversible computation requires free energy that vanishes as the universe approaches thermodynamic equilibrium (heat death).

\begin{theorem}[Heat-Death Corollary]\label{thm:heatdeath}
Let $S$ have infinite theorem set $T_S$, and let a physical discovery process have budget function $B$ with $B_\infty < \infty$ (Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:finite}). Then, regardless of enumeration order:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item the process discovers at most $B_\infty$ theorems over the entire future;
  \item the set of theorems it never discovers is infinite.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At most $B_\infty$ steps are ever completed, each discovering at most one new theorem, so $|D_\infty| \le B_\infty < \infty$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:nonexh} with $N = B_\infty$, the complement $T_S\setminus D_\infty$ is infinite.
\end{proof}

The corollary is independent of the numerical value of $B_\infty$ and of the enumeration order: a cleverer order changes \emph{which} $B_\infty$ theorems are found, never \emph{how many}.

\section{Optimal Order and Cooperation}

\begin{proposition}[Frontier optimality of shortlex]\label{prop:opt}
Among all enumerations of $\Sigma^{*}$, shortlex minimizes the worst-case index at which the last statement of each length is first seen; equivalently it minimizes the steps needed to guarantee discovery of every statement of length $\le \ell$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Seeing all statements of length $\le \ell$ requires listing all $\sum_{j\le\ell} k^j$ of them; shortlex lists exactly these in its first $\sum_{j\le\ell} k^j$ positions, achieving the lower bound. Length is the only monotone complexity measure compatible with finiteness of each length class, forcing length-first ordering for minimax-optimal frontier discovery.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Cooperative discovery]\label{prop:coop}
Let $S_1, S_2$ each have infinite theorem set. Then $T_{S_1}\cup T_{S_2}$, any fair interleaving of their shortlex searches, and (when infinite) $T_{S_1}\cap T_{S_2}$ are countably infinite and non-exhaustible by any finite budget. A fair interleaving discovers any complexity-bounded target region of the union within a bounded factor of the faster individual search.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Unions and infinite intersections of countable sets are countable and remain infinite, so Theorem~\ref{thm:nonexh} applies. Round-robin interleaving performs step $m$ of each search after $O(m)$ combined steps, so a target found at step $m_i$ is reached in $O(\min(m_1,m_2))$ steps.
\end{proof}

\section{A Resource-Bounded Incompleteness Gap}

\begin{theorem}[Resource-bounded gap]\label{thm:gap}
Let $S$ have infinite theorem set $T_S$, and let $B : \N \to \N$ be finite at each $t$. Then for every $t$ the set of theorems not discovered by time $t$ is infinite. If $B$ is bounded (Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:finite}), the set of theorems never discovered is infinite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For fixed $t$, apply Theorem~\ref{thm:nonexh} with $N = B(t) < \infty$; the discovered set is finite and its complement in the infinite $T_S$ is infinite. The bounded case is Theorem~\ref{thm:heatdeath}.
\end{proof}

Even an immortal but energy-limited discoverer faces a permanent, infinite backlog of provable-but-undiscovered truths: unprovability is not required for permanent ignorance; a finite budget suffices.

\section{Discussion}

The loss is collective, not individual: no specific theorem is placed beyond reach; the finite budget merely selects a finite prefix whose identity depends on the chosen order. Sections~3--4 separate timeless mathematical \emph{truth} from physically budgeted \emph{access}; heat death touches only the latter. The linchpin throughout is that each length class $\Sigma^\ell$ is finite---this makes shortlex a well-order with finite ranks, gives discovery indices, forces $|D(N)|\le N$, and singles out length as the optimal sort key.

\section{Open Problems}

\begin{conjecture}[No positive density floor]
For a fixed enumeration and any system whose theorem set has natural density zero, the fraction of the first $N$ statements that are theorems tends to $0$, yet the count of discovered theorems still tends to infinity.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Optimal enumerations minimize worst-case discovery time]
Among all enumerations of $\Sigma^{*}$, shortlex minimizes the index at which the last statement of each length is first seen.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Two-system interleaving preserves non-exhaustibility]
If two systems each prove infinitely many statements, then the union, the (infinite) intersection, and any fair interleaving of their theorem sets remain countably infinite and non-exhaustible; the interleaved frontier of the union is, up to a bounded factor, the better of the two.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Resource-bounded incompleteness gap]
For every budget function $B$ allowing $B(t)$ steps by time $t$, and every infinitely-proving system, the set of theorems never discovered by time $t$ is infinite for every $t$; if $B$ is bounded, the forever-undiscovered set is infinite.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

The theorems of any formal system over a finite alphabet are countably infinite and individually reachable at finite enumeration indices (Theorems~\ref{thm:enum},~\ref{thm:idx}), yet collectively non-exhaustible by any finite budget (Theorem~\ref{thm:nonexh}). With the physical limits of computation and the finiteness of the universe's free-energy budget, the Heat-Death Corollary (Theorem~\ref{thm:heatdeath}) follows: any physical discovery process finds only finitely many of infinitely many theorems, leaving an infinite remainder forever undiscovered. The order of enumeration, not the count, is the true degree of freedom. The heat death of the universe is not the death of mathematics---only the end of our finite budget for reading an infinite book.

\end{document}

